WEBVTT

00:00:00.538 --> 00:00:05.345
You're holding two probes, watching a signal,
and trying to keep a useful record.

00:00:05.625 --> 00:00:07.333
You read a small output.

00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:11.058
But what does that number mean, and what
should you measure next?

00:00:12.808 --> 00:00:15.277
Benchlog starts with a spoken reading.

00:00:15.558 --> 00:00:19.328
This synthetic clip goes through the actual
local Whisper model.

00:00:19.882 --> 00:00:24.201
[Synthetic test voice] At ten kilohertz,
output thirty-one millivolts.

00:00:24.639 --> 00:00:29.346
The interpreted number stays pending, so you
can check or correct it before saving.

00:00:29.626 --> 00:00:33.184
That confirmed observation becomes the
starting evidence.

00:00:34.917 --> 00:00:40.012
This is a resistor-capacitor filter, with the
input held at two volts peak to peak.

00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:43.824
One explanation is that the circuit responds
more slowly.

00:00:44.092 --> 00:00:48.299
The other keeps the expected response but
scales every reading down.

00:00:48.542 --> 00:00:52.337
Benchlog fits both models to the same starting
observation.

00:00:52.592 --> 00:00:56.424
Explaining a known reading is not the same as
predicting a new one.

00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:00.271
[Synthetic test voice] What would tell these
apart?

00:01:00.709 --> 00:01:04.766
The app compares their predictions across a
bounded set of frequencies.

00:01:05.046 --> 00:01:09.976
At eighty hertz, one predicts about one point
seven eight volts, and the other predicts

00:01:09.996 --> 00:01:11.691
zero point one nine seven.

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.966
Those are very different answers.

00:01:14.246 --> 00:01:19.101
Benchlog saves the predictions before the next
measurement, so the explanation cannot

00:01:19.121 --> 00:01:20.791
quietly change afterwards.

00:01:22.518 --> 00:01:24.563
Now comes the new observation.

00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:29.501
[Synthetic test voice] At eighty hertz, output
one point seven eight volts.

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:34.931
After confirmation, one point seven eight
volts is compared with the predictions already

00:01:34.951 --> 00:01:40.509
saved. It is close to the slower-response
model, and far from the scale-only example.

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:45.384
The app keeps the original reading, the
proposed test and the result together.

00:01:47.129 --> 00:01:49.849
That does not prove which component changed.

00:01:50.129 --> 00:01:55.411
Resistance and capacitance appear as a
product, so this model cannot separate them.

00:01:55.666 --> 00:01:58.024
And these are only two possibilities.

00:01:58.304 --> 00:02:01.661
In this separate example, neither prediction
matches.

00:02:01.904 --> 00:02:05.974
The app keeps that uncertainty instead of
forcing a diagnosis.

00:02:07.703 --> 00:02:12.945
This is the idea behind Benchlog: turn a
spoken observation into a reason for the next

00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:18.370
experiment. Speech recognition uses AI; the
predictions and comparisons are

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:24.148
transparent code. You can inspect the receipt,
try the prototype and read the tests.

00:02:24.428 --> 00:02:27.310
The next step is a real student bench trial.
